  "notes": [
   "Target: OG 1.049-1.050, FG approximately 1.009-1.010, approximately 5.2-5.3% ABV, 28 IBU, deep dark Schwarzbier color with ruby highlights, 2.45 volumes CO2.\n\nPROCESS:\n1. Start with RO water and build approximately Ca 50 ppm, Mg 5 ppm, Na 10-15 ppm, SO4 45-50 ppm, Cl 65 ppm.\n2. Put all sodium bicarbonate in the mash; do not add bicarbonate to the sparge water.\n3. Adjust mash to pH 5.30-5.33 measured at room temperature. Actual measured mash pH controls; do not force a bicarbonate number if pH is already correct.\n4. Mash Pilsner, Munich I, Vienna, and Caramunich I at 150 F for 60 minutes.\n5. Add Carafa Special II only for the final 15 minutes of the mash.\n6. Mash out at 168 F for 10 minutes.\n7. Boil 90 minutes.\n8. Add Magnum at 60 minutes remaining, adjusted for actual AA% to target approximately 25.5 IBU from Magnum and about 28 IBU total.\n9. Add Hallertau Mittelfrueh at 10 minutes remaining.\n10. Chill to 48-50 F and oxygenate.\n11. Pitch two fresh sachets of SafLager W-34/70.\n12. Ferment initially at 50 F. Around 50 percent attenuation, allow a gradual rise into the mid-50s F. Near terminal gravity, raise to approximately 62 F for cleanup.\n13. Once gravity and flavor are clean, lower temperature gradually, approximately 3-5 F per day, to 32-34 F.\n14. Lager 4-6 weeks.\n15. Add 2 oz whole medium-roast coffee beans at 35-38 F. Begin tasting around 18 hours. Plan to remove around 24 hours; extend toward 36 hours only if aroma remains under target.\n16. Prepare cold brew separately from approximately 2 oz coffee plus 8 fl oz RO water, steeped cold 14-18 hours and paper-filtered.\n17. Conduct bench trials before dosing the batch: 0, 1, 2, and 3 mL concentrate per 100 mL beer.\n18. Scale only the preferred bench-trial dose to the full batch. The correct amount may be zero if whole-bean conditioning already provides enough coffee character.\n19. Closed transfer and purge all receiving vessels aggressively with CO2. Minimize oxygen exposure after fermentation.\n20. Force carbonate to approximately 2.45 volumes CO2.\n\nCOFFEE SELECTION:\nPrefer fresh, medium-roast washed coffee from Guatemala, Colombia, or Brazil with cocoa, milk chocolate, toasted nut, caramel, and brown-sugar characteristics. Avoid very dark/oily roasts and strongly fruity natural-process coffees.\n\nDESIGN INTENT:\nThe beer should remain unmistakably Schwarzbier: clean, dry, smooth, and highly drinkable. Coffee should lift the cocoa/toast character rather than push the beer toward porter or stout.",
   "Sodium Bicarbonate (Baking Soda): 0.001700971387500 kg (Mash) -- water agent",
   "Calcium Chloride: 0.002381357373703 kg (Mash) -- water agent",
   "Magnesium Sulphate (Epsom Salt): 0.00090718376141067 kg (Mash) -- water agent",
   "Calcium Sulphate (Gypsum): 0.0008504847763225 kg (Mash) -- water agent",
   "Calcium Chloride: 0.0019561149855418 kg (Sparge) -- water agent",
   "Magnesium Sulphate (Epsom Salt): 0.00073708680614617 kg (Sparge) -- water agent",
   "Calcium Sulphate (Gypsum): 0.00070873731360209 kg (Sparge) -- water agent",
   "Whirlfloc: 1 each (Other, Boil)",
   "Yeast Nutrient: 1 tsp (Other, Boil)",
   "Coffee Beans - Medium Roast: 2 oz (Other, Secondary)",
   "2 oz fresh whole medium-roast coffee beans. Add cold at 35-38 F. Begin tasting at 18 hours; plan to remove around 24 hours. Extend toward 36 hours only if aroma remains under target. Prefer washed Guatemala, Colombia, or Brazil with cocoa, nut, caramel, and brown-sugar notes.",
   "Cold Brew Coffee Concentrate: TBD by bench trial (Other, Bottle)",
   "Optional only. Prepare approximately 2 oz coarse-ground coffee in 8 fl oz RO water, steep refrigerated 14-18 hours, then paper-filter. Bench trial finished beer at 0, 1, 2, and 3 mL concentrate per 100 mL beer and scale the preferred dose. The correct full-batch addition may be zero."
  ],
